# The four workload shapes that are ONE line — `ramp` and `hold`, each in both units.
#
# `M224` `B` made a workload an ordinary clause (`D1205`), so each of these is editable in place:
# select the declaration, and the band's workload row carries the profile grid, the unit pair and
# the target/duration fields. Switching `ramp` to `hold` here rewrites one line; switching either
# to `step` rewrites one line into three, which is what `staged-shapes.tflw` is for.
#
# **The unit is the difference worth seeing on the plot.** `users` is closed — five virtual users
# each looping, so arrivals depend on how fast the server answers. `rps` is open — arrivals happen
# at the stated rate whatever has finished. The plan panel under the editor draws both, and they
# are not the same picture.

@catalogue @workload
test "the catalogue holds up as readers arrive"
  ramp to 5 users over 2s
  api GET /products
  expect status equals 200
  threshold p95 duration is less than 2s
  threshold error rate is less than 5%

@catalogue @workload
test "the catalogue holds up at a fixed arrival rate"
  ramp to 20 rps over 2s
  api GET /products
  expect status equals 200
  threshold error rate is less than 5%

@health @workload
test "health answers under a steady four readers"
  hold 4 users for 2s
  api GET /health
  expect status equals 200
  threshold p95 duration is less than 1s
  threshold error rate is less than 5%

@health @workload
test "health answers at a steady fifteen a second"
  hold 15 rps for 2s
  api GET /health
  expect status equals 200
  threshold error rate is less than 5%
